// @test: run
// @tier: 1
// @level: L0
// @tags: aot, codegen, iterator, map, closure, T0260
// @timeout: 180000
// @expected-exit: 0
// @expected-stdout-begin
// skip 2
// take 2
// rev 3
// map 3
// @expected-stdout-end

// `xs.iter().map(f)` SIGSEGV'd at Tier 1. Every closure-FREE adapter
// answered — the axis was measured across five adapters in one
// programme, so nothing else could differ:
//
//     skip(1).count()      2 / 2        ok
//     take(2).count()      2 / 2        ok
//     rev().count()        3 / 3        ok
//     map(|x| *x).count()  3 / SIGSEGV
//
// The cause was an eager-walk INTERCEPTION, added when the generic
// `MappedIter.next` body was unreachable and the lazy path const-zeroed.
// It returns a LIST while the static type of `xs.iter().map(f)` is a
// `MappedIter`, so the first operation reaching a real `MappedIter` body
// reads a List through a MappedIter's field layout — `.next()` loads
// field 0 at +24, which on the List it actually gets is `len`; for
// `[1,2,3]` that is 3, and 3 + 24 = 0x1b, the fault address.
//
// The band-aid carried its own kill switch "so the lazy path can be
// MEASURED rather than argued about". Measured, it is strictly better:
// `.map(f).count()` answers, `.map(f).collect()` refuses BY NAME instead
// of crashing, and `.map(f).sum()` answers where the eager walk silently
// stopped and exited 0 — a wrong result reported as success, which is
// the one failure mode this codebase refuses outright.
//
// The closure-free adapters are in this file as the CONTROL: they never
// reached the intercepted arm, so a change that broke them would mean
// the flip had reached further than its own gate.
//
// `filter` is deliberately NOT here. It is not intercepted by that arm
// at all and behaves identically under both settings — silently stopping
// at Tier 1 — so it is a separate defect this one was masking, and
// pinning it here would attach someone else's failure to this contract.

fn main() {
    let xs: List<Int> = [3, 1, 2];
    print(f"skip {xs.iter().skip(1).count()}");
    print(f"take {xs.iter().take(2).count()}");
    print(f"rev {xs.iter().rev().count()}");
    print(f"map {xs.iter().map(|x| *x).count()}");
}
